our approach.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:55.780
Applications that sit
on-premises or in public cloud.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:58.540
Application is an application
is an application.

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.760
The security requirements
are the same.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:03.490
We have to be paying
much importance

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.250
to what our posture looks
like, regardless whether it's

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:08.050
in public or private cloud.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:11.830
It gave us an opportunity to
look at our existing landscape

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:15.610
and improve our
security postures.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.160
Along with that, there
was tremendous amount

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.950
of effort invested by
my security architect,

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:23.980
by security organization,
to invest effort

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.710
into a secret
management approach.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:28.810
Working through our
cloud native solutions

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:33.880
of offerings that are
available to apply our bring

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:37.630
your own key strategy,
extending our perimeter,

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:41.230
and also just
provide segmentation

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.120
into our environment.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:46.660
That effort resulted
in a very key milestone

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:49.540
that we achieved, where
secret applications based

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:52.330
on data classification
are not just welcome.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:54.760
They're actually
preferred in public cloud.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:58.870
That is a tremendous
achievement from my team,

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:01.580
and the support from
the whole organization.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.280
So that covers the
security aspect of it.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:07.750
What I'd like to go next
is the enterprise agility.

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:09.580
Going back to reducing friction.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:14.830
So just sheer mass
scale of Schlumberger

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:19.300
and the amount of resources that
were migrated into the cloud.

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:22.360
It is incredibly
important to pay attention

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:24.730
to how we deployed
our resources.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.530
How did we organize them?

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:27.970
How did we tag them?

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:29.230
Label them?

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:32.710
It has a significant
impact on what

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.810
happens to our
billing, what happens

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:37.180
to our consistency
in deployment.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:40.810
And it adds organized
chaos, let's just say.

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:44.990
Because we were learning
as we were doing things.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:48.610
And it has helped
us to learn where

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:51.710
we need to stop, think,
stop the bleeding,

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.870
and then keep moving forward.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:57.010
So what we have done is we
have implemented consistency.

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.130
And we have tried our best,
and it's a work in progress,

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:04.270
to introduce automation in
that consistency effort.

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:05.950
My architect and
my team is still

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:09.683
continuously evolving along with
the cloud service providers.

00:15:09.683 --> 00:15:11.350
There were a few
services that were just

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:14.940
made GA last year that we ended
up using for that purpose.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:19.070
And it has been
incredibly helpful.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.310
Next step, cloud transformation.

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:24.280
I think when we talk
about our focused areas,

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:29.430
we cannot ignore cloud
transformation aspect.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:33.220
IaaS VMs is step one.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.640
At this point, if we do not
explore cloud native services,

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.710
PaaS services that
are available already,

00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:40.510
it is an opportunity lost.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:42.250
So one of the things
that my team has

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:45.690
invest that effort, with support
from application teams, is

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.390
do an assessment for our
application landscape

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.580
from the perspective
of modernization.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.390
What does it take to improve
the application stance,

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:56.150
and actually deploy it
in a PaaS environment?

00:15:56.150 --> 00:16:00.370
PaaS offerings that actually
add value to our business.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:02.170
So those are the ones
that we focused on.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:04.200
And we worked with our
cloud service providers

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.960
to meet the gaps, to say,
let's go back to engineering.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.700
Let's ask their hope.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.230
And help them
challenge us to say,

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.230
where do we need to
change our standards,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.180
and where do we need
to go ahead and maybe

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.470
work with them to introduce
new features into the services.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:23.040
That has also tied back
into our security landscape.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:26.010
Because perimeter has
changed in the cloud.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.260
It is no longer just a
firewall-based perimeter.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:32.550
So we had to rethink our
approach from API management

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:35.130
perspective, and what
PaaS environments

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.390
and what controls
do we have to apply.

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:41.130
So it is an accepted service
offering that we can offer.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.770
That being said,
one of the things

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.080
that we have
tremendously focused on

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:52.240
is the automation aspect.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.410
Yes.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.830
You think about how you
deploy these services, what

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:01.240
controls to apply, everyone
jumps to compliance.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:05.050
One of the things that we are
focusing on is consistency.

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:09.290
If we get to a state of
consistent resource deployment,

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:13.040
it will be less of a battle
to get towards compliance.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.819
And not that we're not
working towards it.

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:16.736
We're working with our
cloud service providers

00:17:16.736 --> 00:17:20.680
to find better ways to
meet that compliance.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:24.550
But we have invested tremendous
effort in a few things.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.380
Infrastructure as code.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.830
So templating out
what we are deploying.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:32.170
And with the support
of our operations team,

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:34.780
implementation of configuration
management system.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.210
So every resource
that goes out there

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:42.070
is out there in an efficient,
optimized, and consistent way.

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:44.900
That has helped us tremendously.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:47.560
And I think it will
introduce tremendous amount

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:52.210
of optimization into
the way we work.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.790
And the buy in from operations
and the organization

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:56.920
has helped a lot.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.410
There was a lot of
effort investing

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:03.070
in understanding what tools to
use, what approach to go for.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:07.240
It has been, I think, one of
the most revolutionary changes.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:09.950
And upscaling our folks
to learn infrastructure

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:15.040
as code and the technical
skill that applies to it.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:20.630
That leads into our
service operations piece.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.220
Yes, automation is good.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.740
But you have no idea what to
automate until you actually

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.480
manually try out the process.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:31.040
So one thing that we did is, we
had a consistent cloud hosting

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:32.130
process.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.940
So the work that our operations
team, the delivery team,

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:38.750
was doing, is now exposed to
the rest of the organization

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:41.910
to utilize the services
that the team has to offer.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.210
And it is a simplified
approach, so you

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:47.570
don't have a lot of feels,
a lot of questions to answer

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:50.750
to get resources in cloud.

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:54.650
That is also a work in progress,
as people start learning.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:58.490
And it is a massive organization
to distribute that information

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:00.470
that this service is
actually available.

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.870
My team has done
a tremendous job

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.570
in presenting these offerings,
and services, and the way

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.430
that we are implementing
this process,

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:09.800
and getting the feedback,
and iterating it

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.750
over and over again.

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:14.810
We apply our learnings
as soon as we can.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.030
And we would try to
change as much as we can

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:19.430
as fast as we can.

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:23.090
And these little
building blocks has

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:26.960
helped us put the pieces
and foundation together.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:31.160
So for our service offerings,
we can automate these pieces.

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:33.890
So that is also a work
in progress for us.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:35.750
That when we see
our final vision

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:39.020
that Abuzar will talk next
about, for our service

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:41.600
management and automation
pieces, that how

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.270
these efforts are
lining up for a bigger

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:47.400
vision for our organization.

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:51.300
And lastly, community
of practice.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:52.950
It's very close to my heart.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:56.580
And this is what ties
into change management.

00:19:56.580 --> 00:20:00.480
Our learnings, our
trainings, our sessions,

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.330
our reference architectures
from our PoCs,

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:06.000
for our PaaS environments
and modernizations,

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.570
working with our cloud
service providers

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:13.800
partners, TAMs, learning from
them, getting that information,

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:18.330
getting that training scheduled,
and being proactive about it.

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:22.140
And getting that knowledge
to our teams so it encourages

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:23.460
cloud adoption.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.380
When they take their first
step, they're not scared.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:28.510
They don't know,
where do I go next.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:30.490
How do I get my Google project?

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:33.270
How do I go ahead and
start these services?

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.130
Do we have direction?

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:37.620
Can I go through my
qualification processes

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.930
as I go ahead and
embark on this journey?

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:45.010
The idea is to spread that
knowledge as much as we can.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:49.920
So these pieces, with
our guiding principles

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.620
of cloud first,
with the direction

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.730
to reduce friction, with the
direction to say, yes, we

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.160
will select cloud
native offerings.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:03.150
We have a bias towards it
because we have chose that way.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.520
And we have partnered with
our cloud service providers

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.760
to help reduce friction
in these areas.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:13.020
That is where our efforts,
every work stream, every task,

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:16.590
every discussion, that
is how we make decisions.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.350
For each organization
that goes on this journey,

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.480
your implementation is
going to be different.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.550
Because your landscape
is different.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.310
Ours has changed significantly.

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.480
Our learning, the
way we go around,

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.910
and it's a massive organization.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:32.100
We figure new things
out every day.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.230
But I hope this
actually provides

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:35.580
information and insight.

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:38.370
And if it seems like
a daunting task,

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.860
because it is a daunting task.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:44.010
That being said, I'd like
to call Abuzar back up

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:48.230
on stage for our service
automation management

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:49.330
and automation pace.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:50.420
ABUZAR MANAGER: Great.

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:51.170
Thank you, Sadia.

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:52.870
Thank you very much for that.

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:56.030
So guys, basically,
this piece of work

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:59.300
is our way of sharing,
for those of you who

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:00.800
are going to embark
on this journey,

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.380
or those of you who are
already on this journey,

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:06.620
to know that some of the pain
points that you feel that you

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.560
see are some of the
pain points that most

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:13.310
large organizations will invest
time and effort in learning

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:14.630
and actually building.

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:18.200
What I would like to talk a
bit about in the next slide

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:21.080
is service management
and automation.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:23.540
As a part of our journey, one
of the things that happened

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:27.610
is that we chose to bring
managed services in-house.

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:31.700
Now, for those of you who have
managed services in-house,

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:36.470
you probably know that this
itself is a very large beast

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:38.240
to deal with.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:42.880
Now, when we talk about service
management and automation,

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.850
we are talking about
building the blocks that

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:48.700
will help ensure
that we can support

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.590
the operations of
our applications

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:52.760
once they are residing
in public cloud.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:56.580
And we are trying to
bring those applications,

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:58.240
and we are trying
to also align this

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:02.050
with our core petals of our
savings and optimizations,

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:04.630
our ability to provide
our agility, simplicity,

00:23:04.630 --> 00:23:07.120
and the enablement of
technology, solutions,

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:08.480
and services.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.320
So I will break this
up into two core areas.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:13.420
For those of you who are
already on this journey,

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:17.140
you probably will be able
to associate with us.

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:21.070
One of the major areas is for
us to be able to build a service

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:22.420
catalog.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:26.260
Basically, a understanding of
what our IT services are going

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:28.690
to be, what are our core
services that we need

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:32.020
to manage, because of our
applications primarily residing

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.300
in the infrastructure
as a service,

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:39.075
and then subsequently moving
into PaaS and other SAS

00:23:39.075 --> 00:23:40.450
offerings of the
public cloud, it

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:43.000
was important for us to
focus on what we have

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:45.430
and what we need to support
in infrastructure as a service

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:46.580
today.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.760
So as a result, the need
for a service catalog

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:49.960
became imperative.

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:52.372
It was important
for us to know what

00:23:52.372 --> 00:23:54.580
is it that we are going to
support, but not only what

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:57.490
are we going to support for our
internal customers, but also

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:00.550
how are we going to support and
measure this against the SLAs.

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:03.190
So the journey was to
build a catalog that

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:07.060
will allow us to understand
that, promote that, and be

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:11.590
able to advocate our services
to our wider organization.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:13.840
At the same time, build the
knowledge and the know how

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:16.360
of how those services
are going to be supported

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:18.250
with the growing
technology and the changing

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:23.435
technology with our public
cloud service providers.

00:24:23.435 --> 00:24:25.060
One of those things
that I have learned

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:29.370
over the last 2 and 1/2 years
is that, it's like a six week

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.080
sprint, if not lesser.

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:34.560
Where, by the time
you have a problem,

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:37.770
there is an solution,
most likely, in alpha.

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:39.180
So you have to be ready.

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:40.180
You have to be prepared.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:42.990
You have to be adopting
and adapting these

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:45.360
to be able to provide
these as a service

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.730
to your larger organization.

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:50.070
With the service management,
one of the core things

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:56.140
that we have now is a dynamic
and a growing service catalog

00:24:56.140 --> 00:24:58.870
that allows us to be able
to support applications

00:24:58.870 --> 00:25:03.083
across the fleet in terms
of what services can

00:25:03.083 --> 00:25:05.500
we offer with our backup and
restoration, and some of that

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:09.260
we will talk about in our
operations discussion.

00:25:09.260 --> 00:25:11.300
And then, as we
journeyed along, one

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:12.860
of the bigger
objectives we had was

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:16.900
to be able to achieve savings.

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:19.450
And savings in the sense,
tangible dollar value savings,

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:21.310
that we can show
to our leadership

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.650
as a part of moving
from our legacy data

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:25.185
centers into public cloud.

00:25:25.185 --> 00:25:26.560
One of those things
that you will

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:29.470
see in one of the other
slides that are to come

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:31.630
is that, it is heavily
perceived that when

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:35.020
you move to public cloud, that
you start saving instantly.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:36.760
Now, one of those
things that you will--

00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:38.260
for those of you
who've already been

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:42.970
there will know that, savings is
something that you can achieve.

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:43.930
You will achieve.

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:45.730
But it requires a
level of education.

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.350
It requires a
level of commitment

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.840
in terms of optimizing,
and more importantly,

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:54.100
automating your services to be
able to achieve those savings

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:55.330
and actually realize them.

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:58.060
Because your usage in
cloud, in public cloud,

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:00.070
is so dynamic that
if you're not aware,

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.170
if you're not cognizant
of this very quickly,

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.820
you will start burning
holes into your savings.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:07.990
So with service management and
putting a catalog together,

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.210
it was important for us to
start looking at how do we

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:14.230
automate some of those services
to be able to truly convert

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:17.350
what we were seeing into
tangible dollar values.

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:20.350
So, you know, automation
is quite widely spoken

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:22.152
and abused terminology.

00:26:22.152 --> 00:26:24.610
When we talk about automation,
what we are talking about is

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:29.180
some simple ways of how we can
convert today that we cannot do

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:32.350
in our legacy data centers,
and is readily available

00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:34.778
in our public cloud, to
true dollar value savings.

00:26:34.778 --> 00:26:36.820
So an example that I would
like to share with you

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:40.450
is our ability to measure our
billings over a three month

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:43.960
cycle, and then optimize what
our configurations need to be.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:45.460
And this is really
basic and simple.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.520
So today, if you have an
application that has a month

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:52.333
end, or quarter end, or
a year end requirement,

00:26:52.333 --> 00:26:54.250
in your legacy data
centers, what you will see

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:55.875
is that those
applications are actually

00:26:55.875 --> 00:27:00.280
built, configured, and
specced to meet those once

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:03.700
off deliverables.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:07.180
What you can do in cloud is,
you can actually program.

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:09.550
You can automate this
in a very simple way

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:11.500
to ramp it up at
month end and ramp it

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:12.970
down again after month end.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.310
To be able to pay
those high compute,

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:18.790
high storage costs only when
you are actually using them,

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:21.880
and not for the entire
period that the application

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:22.670
is running.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:25.690
So this was one of those
areas, simple areas where we've

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:30.160
been able to do automate
our presence in public cloud

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:35.410
and be able to save on the
compute and the operations

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:37.760
cost of those applications.

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:39.710
This is a journey that
is going to continue.

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:42.340
There is a lot more
that we can and we

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:44.980
will continue in
the automation space

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:48.910
to be able to achieve our
financial savings of being

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:50.180
in the public cloud.

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:52.600
But more importantly, it
is also about how do we

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:55.637
optimize the presence
of those applications.

00:27:55.637 --> 00:27:57.220
One of the other
examples that we have

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:59.560
is that there are applications
that we run that have

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:00.730
different environments.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:03.930
And most organizations
will be familiar with this

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:06.670
that, for a good set
of your applications,

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:10.630
you would have your dev test and
your production environments.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.400
What public cloud
will allow you to do

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:14.140
is, you can automate
the shutdowns

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:16.180
of your non-production
environments,

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:19.450
and be able to optimize
on the cost savings there

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:23.690
by not having to pay for those
when you're not using them.

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:25.990
So these are some of the
learnings that we've had,

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:28.480
some of the stuff that
we've sort of gone through.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:30.340
And we've journeyed
along and we realized

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:33.610
that this is where
the true rubber hits

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:36.460
the road when it comes to your
savings and optimizations.

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.770
At the same time,
with automation,

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.690
it gives us the ability
to provide agility

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:47.710
if there is a concept, an idea,
that requires an infrastructure

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:51.820
to be able to test, to
be able to evaluate.

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:53.500
The ability to do
that in public cloud

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.570
has been a lot greater than
it has been in the legacy data

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:57.070
centers.

00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:01.810
For anyone who's who sort of
had outsourced data centers,

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:03.370
you probably know
that getting a VM

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:05.670
can take anywhere
between two to six weeks.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:08.740
That is, if you have the right
processes and right automation

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:12.180
tools within your cloud
management system.

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:18.860
You would be able to do that
in days, if not in hours.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:20.530
So what this, basically--

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:22.870
the focus for the service
management and automation

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:24.520
pillar, which was
a dedicated pillar,

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:27.800
wants to be able to build that
catalog and, at the same time,

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:32.080
convert all of those services as
microservices that can then be

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:34.840
tackled and then be automated.

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:36.580
The question should
be, what do we

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:40.850
do when you are in a
multi cloud environment,

00:29:40.850 --> 00:29:44.800
and you need to be able
to not only automate,

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:48.040
but you also need to be able to
monitor your different services

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:51.610
and offerings across
a variety of clouds?

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:54.100
This was something that
we've sort of learned.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:58.210
And in hindsight,
there are things

00:29:58.210 --> 00:29:59.418
that can be done differently.

00:29:59.418 --> 00:30:01.835
But one of the things that we
have done is, we've gone out

00:30:01.835 --> 00:30:04.000
and we said, we will build
a tool that will give us

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:08.320
the ability to absorb APIs from
our various cloud environments

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:11.110
and bring it into a central
portal where we can not only

00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:15.160
have a visualization for
our application owners,

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:18.778
subsequently our leadership
to be able to see how

00:30:18.778 --> 00:30:20.320
the applications
are performing, what

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:25.810
the compute, what the cost for
the storage, the licensing,

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:28.480
and the various components
of the application are.

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:31.510
But at the same time,
be able to then say,

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:34.540
look at what are our high
frequency, complex services

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:37.240
that we need to do to
provide to our applications,

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:40.480
and how can we start targeting
automation around that?

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:43.720
So we've achieved a
certain level of automation

00:30:43.720 --> 00:30:47.320
that has allowed us to achieve
a certain level of dollar value

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.840
savings, which is
aligned with the journey

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.160
that we want to proceed.

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:55.060
And this, in 2019, is going
to be our focus areas as we

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:58.060
journey along the
modernization and we continue

00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:01.382
to optimize on our automation.

00:31:01.382 --> 00:31:03.340
One of those things, like
I said, in hindsight,

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:04.882
that could have been
done differently

00:31:04.882 --> 00:31:10.120
is, look at what SAS offerings
and other tooling methods

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:12.350
are available in the
market out there.

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:15.310
And it is growing every day.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.140
That can allow you
that flexibility.

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:19.330
So that is one
thing that I think

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:25.990
is going to be a focus area for
organizations that are either

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:27.940
embarking on this journey
or are already there

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:30.357
and are looking to see how
they can optimize their managed

00:31:30.357 --> 00:31:31.532
services.

00:31:31.532 --> 00:31:32.990
What I would like
to do is, I would

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:38.600
like to sort of bring our
operations manager, Sheri

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:42.950
Wheatley, to come and share
with us some of her findings

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:47.060
and learnings that has helped
make Schlumberger's cloud

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.560
operations a better place today.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:50.200
Sheri?

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:51.283
SHERI WHEATLEY: Thank you.

00:31:51.283 --> 00:31:53.190
I'll change the slide.

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:53.690
Hello.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.560
Thank you for joining
our session today.

00:31:55.560 --> 00:31:57.480
I want to talk a little
bit about operations

00:31:57.480 --> 00:31:59.960
and what we're doing when we
started in pretty much early

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:03.170
2018 through where
we are today, right?

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:06.770
So our vision was to
bring cloud ops in-house,

00:32:06.770 --> 00:32:10.130
moving away from our providers,
so that we could make sure

00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:14.870
that we support and stabilize
the applications that we're

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:18.060
migrating over to the
multi cloud providers.

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:21.170
And that basically aligns
with our corporate and digital

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:22.380
strategy.

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:23.540
So we started that.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:26.460
We also started
evaluating tools,

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:28.640
automation orchestration
cloud tooling.

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:31.310
We knew we needed something to
help with the cloud operations

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:32.930
team.

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:34.140
And people, right?

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:35.460
People were very important.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:36.620
We had to figure out--

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:40.130
we had seniors,
guys in our group.

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:42.020
They were very, very good.

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:44.750
We also wanted to bring
in cloud knowledge.

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:48.722
So we looked for specific
skill sets and capabilities

00:32:48.722 --> 00:32:50.930
and made sure, too, we could
handle the capacity that

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:53.400
was coming our way.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:55.600
When it comes to
our data centers,

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:58.470
we have legacy data centers,
multi, we have colos.

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:01.230
And we're working on assessing
where we could actually

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:03.960
start consolidating, and
closing down, and moving

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:06.850
a lot of these applications,
again, to the cloud.

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:10.080
So when we moved into
2019, we actually

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:13.800
started implementing the
tooling that we did the POC on.

00:33:13.800 --> 00:33:16.020
That orchestration
automation, hopefully it's

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.790
going to gain efficiencies.

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:19.770
And the plan is to
reduce cost as well.

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:22.420
And to optimize on the capacity
and the size of, you know,

00:33:22.420 --> 00:33:23.880
the VMs.

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:25.870
We also line our catalog.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:28.800
So we ensure that we're going
to work through our SOAs

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:32.200
to meet our customer
requirements and the business.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:35.670
And at that time, too, we're
also reorganizing cloud ops

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:39.120
to actually join the
applications team, so

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.970
that Google has been
spending quite a lot of time

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:47.078
with us, so that we could
adopt the SRE model and DevOps.

00:33:47.078 --> 00:33:48.870
So that's what we're
working through, which

00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:51.063
we're very excited to do that.

00:33:51.063 --> 00:33:52.980
More and more of the
high valued applications,

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:54.230
we're moving into the cloud.

00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:56.700
So we are also setting
up the follow-the-sun.

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:58.980
We've got critical
applications that

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.530
need to be supported
around the clock.

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:04.180
Tech debt obsolescence.

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:05.702
That's a big project this year.

00:34:05.702 --> 00:34:07.660
I think a lot of people
are going through that.

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:09.370
And so we're working
on that, figuring

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:10.900
out what can we modernize.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:13.100
What do we need to do?

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:14.530
Can we decommission?

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:16.330
And yesterday, really
exciting news from

00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:19.449
Google that we can consider
using the Anthos tooling.

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:21.429
So we're looking into that.

00:34:21.429 --> 00:34:24.245
And then one other area that
we need to really work on,

00:34:24.245 --> 00:34:26.120
this is something that
we've been struggling,

00:34:26.120 --> 00:34:28.270
and I think we finally
have a solution,

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:30.130
and that's our
monitoring, right?

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:33.130
We can't do well in the cloud
without a good application

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:35.739
that we can monitor on alerts.

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:37.883
And then last, we're
going to continue.

00:34:37.883 --> 00:34:38.800
It's a journey, right?

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:40.383
Another 12, 18 months,
but we're going

00:34:40.383 --> 00:34:43.540
to continue working on reducing
our internal data centers

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:44.986
on-prem.

00:34:44.986 --> 00:34:46.500
So next, I want to
talk a little bit

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:51.040
about where we feel we are
today with cloud readiness.

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:52.500
There's five core areas.

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:54.810
First two, the patching
and the backup, right?

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:58.080
Those two solutions had to be in
place before we could roll out

00:34:58.080 --> 00:34:59.430
anything into the cloud.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:02.610
The good news is, we were
able to leverage and extend

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:06.630
our patching and our
backup tooling from on-prem

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:07.740
to the cloud.

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:08.790
So that worked out well.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.130
Provisioning,
we're almost there.

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:14.880
But again, until we start using
that cloud automation tooling,

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:17.070
we're still running some
scripts and deploying VMs.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:19.890
So we want to be able to bring
those quicker to the market.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:23.640
And then we're looking
to working through

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:25.330
on disaster recovery.

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:26.520
It's more of Schlumberger.

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:29.130
I mean, DR. It's there,
right in the cloud.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.800
But we spent a
great deal of time.

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:34.560
You know, security requirements,
working with the teams.

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:36.300
We had to deal with
the data residency.

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:37.770
We had to deal
with the sensitive

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:39.490
and the confidential data.

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:42.540
So that's kind of slowed
is down, but we're there.

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:45.830
We're now able to deploy
those systems in the clouds.

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:46.920
So our architecture team--

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:49.470
Sadia, the lead, she'll
be working on bringing

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:52.510
us a solution later this year.

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:54.800
And last, I want to talk a
little bit about security.

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:56.800
I think everybody knows
it's always a challenge.

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:57.850
You know, every
time we turn around,

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.140
there's new vulnerabilities.

00:35:59.140 --> 00:36:00.557
You know, how do
we capture these?

00:36:00.557 --> 00:36:02.240
How do we patch
these quick enough?

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:05.050
So we've partnered with
our security teams.

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:08.200
And they're working with us
to bring us some tooling,

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:12.670
simplify some of the process,
deliver reporting, as well

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:13.390
as dashboards.

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:14.890
We need that visibility.

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:16.660
And it's important
not just for the team,

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:19.030
but for the
management in general.

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:20.390
So that's where we're at.

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:22.743
And I'll turn it
back over to Abuzar.

00:36:22.743 --> 00:36:23.660
ABUZAR MANAGER: Great.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:24.368
Thank you, Sheri.

00:36:24.368 --> 00:36:27.030
SADIA MUKHTAR: You're welcome.

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:30.260
ABUZAR MANAGER: So guys,
again, just recapping on this.

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:35.450
For those organizations that
have operations in-house,

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:38.750
it is one of those
areas where, I

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:42.870
think I can say this as
candidly as possible,

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:46.730
it is the most
thankless area to be in.

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:49.340
It is an area where you
have to keep the lights on

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:54.570
and you have to continue to
keep doing more with less.

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:57.650
What Sheri has shared with us
is that, along the journey,

00:36:57.650 --> 00:37:02.240
since 2017 as we started to
move the first application

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:05.240
and had to start supporting it,
there was a barrage of things

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:06.860
that we had to take
on board, and we

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:10.460
had to make sure that we
were not only equipped

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:13.310
in terms of our capabilities,
but also from our capacity

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:15.380
point of view, to
be able to support

00:37:15.380 --> 00:37:17.630
some of those things
that were previously

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:22.010
being done by an outside or an
outsourced service provider.

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:25.010
What you see in
the quadrant where

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:27.020
we are talking about
our cloud readiness,

00:37:27.020 --> 00:37:30.380
one of the things that I
think we want to elaborate on

00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:32.870
is that, this is our--

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:34.880
unlike any other organization.

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:37.970
Sometimes, you are the
victim of your own processes.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.820
And we have been through that
journey, especially when it

00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:43.040
comes around data
residency, when

00:37:43.040 --> 00:37:45.980
it comes around security,
when it comes around areas

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:47.340
where you can actually--

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:50.840
where you're dealing
with the client data,

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:53.780
it puts some limitations around
what you can and cannot do.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:56.480
What you see over there
is what that journey

00:37:56.480 --> 00:37:58.700
has been in 2017 onwards.

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:04.550
For Schlumberger to grow its
readiness in those five core

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:08.800
areas, in terms of being
able to adopt and adapt what

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:11.210
the offerings have been
from the native cloud.

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:13.940
At the same time,
there are areas

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:17.030
where we had to go and build
certain solutions because

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:21.390
of the lack of those solutions
meeting our own requirements,

00:38:21.390 --> 00:38:24.650
especially in the backup and the
restoration space, where we had

00:38:24.650 --> 00:38:27.080
very specific application
requirements that

00:38:27.080 --> 00:38:28.192
had to be catered to.

00:38:28.192 --> 00:38:29.150
So it's been a journey.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:31.320
And this is important
to call out,

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:33.860
because the growth
that you see today,

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:37.490
the ability that we have, and
the presence that we have today

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:40.370
for an organization like
as large as Schlumberger

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:43.010
with as archaic
applications, and processes,

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:45.290
and technology
debt that we have,

00:38:45.290 --> 00:38:47.360
to be able to comfortably
stand up and say, yes.

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:50.060
We have a very strong footing
today in a multi cloud

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:53.240
environment to the position
where we can actually

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:56.570
stand and share our learnings
with you has required

00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:59.000
us to work very closely
with our partners,

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:02.510
with our multi cloud service
partners, our application

00:39:02.510 --> 00:39:04.190
development partners,
to make sure

00:39:04.190 --> 00:39:07.070
that this is a robust
footing for our future state,

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:08.900
our modernization,
and the journey

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:11.220
that we want to carry
on where we can start

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:14.720
to PaaS our applications, and
then subsequently containerize

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:16.640
them.

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:18.560
This slide is a
slide that I think

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:23.300
anyone who has ever worked
with a large organization,

00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:27.090
no matter what project you
are on, can associate with.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:30.080
So what-- and I'm not going to
go through every one of these.

00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:33.320
But one of the challenges
that, if you are already

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:36.127
on the journey, or if you
are starting this journey,

00:39:36.127 --> 00:39:38.210
or if you're thinking about
starting this journey,

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.940
and there are certain
areas which are daunting,

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:44.435
food for thought is that
there are certain competing

00:39:44.435 --> 00:39:46.560
priorities that you would
always have to deal with.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:47.602
And we were no different.

00:39:47.602 --> 00:39:49.400
There were multiple
large programs

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.410
that were running in
parallel that were

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:53.810
sucking most of our resources.

00:39:53.810 --> 00:39:56.690
As a result of which,
capability of being

00:39:56.690 --> 00:40:00.740
able to do this in a quicker,
in a more effective manner,

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:02.510
was compromised.

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:04.640
There is a perception
that cloud is cheap.

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:06.990
But like I said before,
it comes at a cost.

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:09.380
It comes at an awareness
and a learning.

00:40:09.380 --> 00:40:11.930
And we need to be
mindful of that.

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:13.850
Security is, again,
one of those areas

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:17.630
that every organization will
have some challenges around.

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:18.740
Knowledge and bandwidth.

00:40:18.740 --> 00:40:21.980
Again, as you journey into
cloud and the rate at which

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:24.300
our public and private
cloud is growing,

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:26.870
you will realize that this
is a constant learning.

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:31.580
And as Sadia pointed out, the
areas where we are focusing in

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:36.890
is making sure that as we grow
the skills with our teams,

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:39.530
we grow the skills
through proper training,

00:40:39.530 --> 00:40:43.670
and partnering with our
cloud service providers.

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:45.788
License validation and budget.

00:40:45.788 --> 00:40:47.580
Yes, there's always a
constraint of budget.

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:50.570
I don't think there's anyone
in the room who will deny that.

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:53.090
You know, money is
abundance and that you could

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:54.710
go out and do what you want to.

00:40:54.710 --> 00:40:58.970
So there's always a need for
us to be able to play smart

00:40:58.970 --> 00:40:59.670
in that space.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:01.420
And it's going to be
one of the challenges

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:03.640
that we have to deal with.

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:05.900
And I think this
sort of basically

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:10.290
brings us to the
slide where, it's

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:12.930
a lighter way of
putting together

00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:18.180
what our journey has looked like
since we started the journey.

00:41:18.180 --> 00:41:22.560
It was the perception that the
leadership team mostly have is

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:25.305
that, moving to cloud is
snap of a finger, right?

00:41:25.305 --> 00:41:26.430
You pick your applications.

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:27.013
You move them.

00:41:27.013 --> 00:41:28.727
But when you go
through the journey,

00:41:28.727 --> 00:41:29.810
there's a lot of learning.

00:41:29.810 --> 00:41:34.020
So this slide basically
helps me summarize the blurb

00:41:34.020 --> 00:41:38.710
that we've just been going on
for the last 42 minutes now.

00:41:38.710 --> 00:41:42.480
In a nutshell, it does
require a significant amount

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:46.320
of preplanning so that your
efforts during your migration,

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:48.840
your failures during the
journey of actually moving

00:41:48.840 --> 00:41:51.580
the applications, are
significantly reduced.

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:53.160
So the area where
I think everyone

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:56.040
who's embarking on this
journey should focus on

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:57.150
is a detailed assessment.

00:41:57.150 --> 00:42:00.060
It's imperative that you know
what you're dealing with.

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:03.660
It's imperative
that you know what

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:05.940
is the workload that you're
carrying with you, what

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:07.770
is the debt that you're
carrying with you,

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:09.930
so that you can make
a fair assessment.

00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:15.300
It's important to understand
that cloud first is a strategy

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:17.160
that most organizations have.

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:18.910
Gartner promotes that as well.

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:24.120
But at the same time, cloud, if
right, should not be overseen.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:27.360
What I mean by that is that
you, as an organization,

00:42:27.360 --> 00:42:30.190
would know what is the
lifespan of your applications.

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:32.550
What is the future state
of your applications.

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:34.830
And that, through your
assessment process,

00:42:34.830 --> 00:42:37.110
will help you qualify
whether the application is

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:38.750
ready for public
cloud, and whether you

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:40.500
want to make the amount
of investment that

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:44.190
would be required in some
cases to actually cut across

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:46.030
the line on that side.

00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:49.320
The other thing that we learned
as a part of the journey

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:53.670
is that it is important to
go down a proof of concept

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:55.480
to be able to
validate two things.

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:58.570
One, how simple,
complex, or super complex

00:42:58.570 --> 00:42:59.910
your applications are.

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:02.797
And secondly, what is
the right methodology

00:43:02.797 --> 00:43:03.630
for your migrations?

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:04.588
Is it a lift and shift?

00:43:04.588 --> 00:43:05.670
Or is it a rebuild?

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:06.330
Greenfield?

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:07.350
Or is it a combination?

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:08.490
What is it?

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:10.960
Because this will help
you with two main things.

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:14.490
One, you'll be able to plan
and have a roadmap based

00:43:14.490 --> 00:43:19.080
on your capacity constraints,
with your competing priorities,

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:21.540
your budgeting constraints,
your licensing constraint,

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:23.790
et cetera, on what that
roadmap is going to look like.

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:25.410
But at the same
time, it will help

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:28.117
you partner in with the
right integration partners.

00:43:28.117 --> 00:43:29.700
So one of those
things that we learned

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:31.533
as a journey is that
we did not have all the

00:43:31.533 --> 00:43:33.778
know of the tools,
of the people,

00:43:33.778 --> 00:43:35.820
and we needed to partner
in with the right people

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:38.720
to help us make that journey.

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:40.850
As you journey
through that, you--

00:43:40.850 --> 00:43:45.260
in 2017, once we had completed
a thorough proof of concept,

00:43:45.260 --> 00:43:49.430
we'd done our assessment, we'd
done a good set of applications

00:43:49.430 --> 00:43:51.920
that gave us a level
of comfort, 2017

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:56.520
was more on taking a substantial
amount of our landscape

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:58.160
and actually
running that through

00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:02.540
to be able to validate that
our core vision around savings

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:05.300
and optimizations,
agility, simplicity,

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:08.750
and enablement of
technology, was actually

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:09.660
going to be achieved.

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:12.800
So this was just us
re-validating that that was all

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:14.330
still deliverable.

00:44:14.330 --> 00:44:17.240
And 2018 was then, you roll.

00:44:17.240 --> 00:44:19.410
So that is when we
were very focused.

00:44:19.410 --> 00:44:23.030
We had we had our
objectives aligned

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:24.800
across the organization,
and the focus

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:28.370
was on killing our data
center and moving into cloud.

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:31.400
And 2018 and 2019 was
about building our roadmap

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:33.150
for what the future
is going to look like.

00:44:33.150 --> 00:44:35.210
How are we going to focus
on our modernization,

00:44:35.210 --> 00:44:38.210
and how are we going to take
the journey into now exploiting

00:44:38.210 --> 00:44:40.530
what the cloud has to offer.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:46.040
So basically, on a light
note, this is where we are.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:49.220
I would like to share a
picture of the team that

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:53.120
has been the backbone.

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:54.530
Not everyone is over here.

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:58.010
But this is the team that
has helped significantly

00:44:58.010 --> 00:44:58.787
work with us.

00:44:58.787 --> 00:45:01.120
On this slide, I also want
to take the opportunity today

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:02.773
to stand here.

00:45:02.773 --> 00:45:04.190
We wouldn't be
standing here and I

00:45:04.190 --> 00:45:06.110
wouldn't have had
the opportunity

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:09.050
to be here if it was, A,
not because of the team that

00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:11.660
is right behind me,
literally at the moment,

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:12.870
to support this journey.

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:15.780
But also, I want to
thank our partners.

00:45:15.780 --> 00:45:18.680
And I would like to name a
few of them who've been very

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:20.570
pivotal in the journey with us.

00:45:20.570 --> 00:45:22.700
To start off at
Google, who's partnered

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:24.620
with us throughout the journey.

00:45:24.620 --> 00:45:27.740
And their engineering has
helped us support and build

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:30.410
for our present and
our future state.

00:45:30.410 --> 00:45:32.960
We work very closely with our
cloud providers in Microsoft.

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:36.830
We've worked with Capgemini,
KPMG, Atos, Hitachi,

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:37.890
to name a few.

00:45:37.890 --> 00:45:39.890
And without this support,
without their support,

00:45:39.890 --> 00:45:43.790
without their partnering, and
as I said at the start of it,

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:46.280
this is the wealth
that we've built.

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:47.910
These are the our resources.

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:49.410
These are the people
who've actually

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:53.000
brought the skills, the
team, the tools, and more

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:56.420
importantly, the attitude.

00:45:56.420 --> 00:45:59.330
The can do attitude to
make this a success.

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:00.830
So I want to thank
the people who've

00:46:00.830 --> 00:46:02.640
been a part of this journey.

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:06.103
I know that we've sort of
shared a lot of the stuff

00:46:06.103 --> 00:46:07.520
that you've probably
heard before.

00:46:07.520 --> 00:46:10.543
But if there's anything that we
can share or leave with people

00:46:10.543 --> 00:46:11.960
who are embarking
on this journey,

00:46:11.960 --> 00:46:13.460
we'll be more than
happy to do that.

